  "client_view_text": " The write-ahead log is a mechanism that ensures the database remains consistent even in the case of a crash.\nThe write-ahead log is a mechanism that ensures the database remains consistent even in the case of a crash.\nWhen a database modification is made, it is written into the write-ahead log first (hence the name). This log is a record of all the modifications that have been made, and it is kept in a separate location from the actual database. At the same time, the modification is also applied to the database itself.\nIf a crash occurs before the modification is fully written to the database, the write-ahead log can be used to \n\n## Step 1: Understanding the Write-Ahead Log\nA write-ahead log (WAL) is a mechanism used by databases to ensure data consistency and durability in case of a system crash or failure. It records every modification made to the database before it is written to the main database storage, hence the name \"write-ahead.\"\n\n## Step 2: The Role of the Write-Ahead Log in Recovery\nAfter a database crash, the WAL becomes crucial for recovery. The database uses the WAL to redo any transactions that were not fully written to the database before the crash. This process involves recovering the database to a consistent state by replaying the transactions recorded in the WAL.\n\n## Step 3: Redoing Uncommitted Transactions\nThe database starts by recovering the last consistent checkpoint, which is a known good state of the database. Then, it applies the transactions from the WAL. If a transaction was not fully written to the database before the crash, the database replays that transaction as if it had been committed. This ensures that all such transactions are applied consistently and correctly, maintaining the integrity of the database.\n\n## Step 4: Ensuring Consistency and Durability\nThrough the use of a WAL, the database ensures that even in the event of a system failure"
